1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,974
Lesson 6.1.

2
00:00:02,354 --> 00:00:06,616
Linear systems from the first
moment of an OU process.

3
00:00:07,717 --> 00:00:12,581
Solving a linear system is perhaps the
most common task in scientific computing.

4
00:00:12,961 --> 00:00:17,255
A digital computer does it with
factorizations or with conjugate gradient.

5
00:00:17,635 --> 00:00:22,927
Today we see another way: build a physical
system whose equilibrium is the solution,

6
00:00:22,927 --> 00:00:27,401
let it relax in the presence of
thermal noise and average its position.

7
00:00:27,781 --> 00:00:30,770
It is the central idea of
thermodynamic linear algebra.

8
00:00:31,883 --> 00:00:35,000
Take a symmetric positive
definite matrix A.

9
00:00:35,380 --> 00:00:42,094
We define the potential
U(x) = ½ xᵀ A x − bᵀ x.

10
00:00:42,474 --> 00:00:48,837
Its gradient is A x − b, so its
only minimum is exactly x = A⁻¹ b.

11
00:00:49,217 --> 00:00:52,129
Physically, it is a
set of coupled harmonic

12
00:00:52,129 --> 00:00:56,057
oscillators: A sets the springs
and b the external forces.

13
00:00:57,183 --> 00:01:03,090
In contact with a heat bath, the position
obeys the overdamped Langevin equation: an

14
00:01:03,090 --> 00:01:08,223
Ornstein-Uhlenbeck process that pushes
toward the minimum and adds noise.

15
00:01:08,603 --> 00:01:15,610
At equilibrium, x follows a Gaussian
with mean A⁻¹ b and covariance β⁻¹ A⁻¹.

16
00:01:15,990 --> 00:01:19,557
The solution of the system is the
first moment of the distribution.

17
00:01:19,937 --> 00:01:22,379
The noise is not in the
way: it is what guarantees

18
00:01:22,379 --> 00:01:24,772
that the system explores
and reaches equilibrium.

19
00:01:25,883 --> 00:01:27,743
The protocol has three steps.

20
00:01:28,123 --> 00:01:32,547
First, load A and b into the
couplings and forces of the device.

21
00:01:32,927 --> 00:01:37,734
Second, wait an equilibration
time t₀, of order κ τ_r times a

22
00:01:37,734 --> 00:01:43,328
logarithm, where κ is the condition
number and τ_r the relaxation time.

23
00:01:43,708 --> 00:01:49,473
Third, time-average x over a
window τ with an analog integrator.

24
00:01:49,853 --> 00:01:52,405
That average is the
estimate of the solution.

25
00:01:53,517 --> 00:01:56,475
Let's simulate a Wishart
matrix of dimension

26
00:01:56,475 --> 00:01:59,232
fifty, with condition number thirty-four.

27
00:01:59,612 --> 00:02:04,147
With k_B T = 0.01, after equilibrating,
the relative error of the

28
00:02:04,147 --> 00:02:08,612
average is seven percent with a
window of ten units, two percent

29
00:02:08,612 --> 00:02:12,450
with a hundred and below one
percent with nine hundred.

30
00:02:12,830 --> 00:02:16,097
If we cool ten times, the
error falls about three times:

31
00:02:16,097 --> 00:02:18,956
with a hundred units it
drops to six thousandths.

32
00:02:19,336 --> 00:02:22,358
The error scales as one
over the square root of β τ.

33
00:02:23,483 --> 00:02:27,073
The paper by Aifer and
colleagues gives the exact times.

34
00:02:27,453 --> 00:02:33,185
In the overdamped regime,
the required window

35
00:02:33,185 --> 00:02:38,025
is τ = 2κ²d‖A‖ τ_r / (β‖b‖²ε²(1 − P)).

36
00:02:38,405 --> 00:02:41,185
It grows with the dimension,
with the square of the

37
00:02:41,185 --> 00:02:44,346
condition number and with the
inverse square of the error.

38
00:02:44,726 --> 00:02:50,097
That gives a complexity of order
d κ² ε⁻², against d² √κ for

39
00:02:50,097 --> 00:02:55,557
conjugate gradient: the advantage
appears at large dimension.

40
00:02:56,683 --> 00:03:00,767
This solver shines when a fast
approximate solution is enough:

41
00:03:00,767 --> 00:03:05,114
inside iterative methods, in Newton
steps, in the natural gradient

42
00:03:05,114 --> 00:03:09,132
we will see in unit eight, or
in Gaussian process regression.

43
00:03:09,512 --> 00:03:14,036
In lab nine you will compare it with
conjugate gradient and with Cholesky.

44
00:03:15,150 --> 00:03:18,137
Common mistakes: averaging from time zero,

45
00:03:18,137 --> 00:03:20,910
when the transient biases the estimate.

46
00:03:21,290 --> 00:03:24,388
Using a matrix that is not
positive definite without

47
00:03:24,388 --> 00:03:27,963
transforming the system, which
squares the condition number.

48
00:03:28,343 --> 00:03:30,950
Expecting high precision,
when the error falls

49
00:03:30,950 --> 00:03:33,273
only as one over the square root of time.

50
00:03:33,653 --> 00:03:35,715
And ignoring the cost
of loading the matrix.

51
00:03:36,817 --> 00:03:41,813
In summary: with the right quadratic
potential, the thermal equilibrium of

52
00:03:41,813 --> 00:03:46,539
coupled oscillators is a Gaussian
centered on the solution of A x = b.

53
00:03:46,919 --> 00:03:49,831
One equilibrates,
time-averages, and the error

54
00:03:49,831 --> 00:03:52,427
falls as one over the square root of β τ.

55
00:03:52,807 --> 00:03:57,032
The complexity is of order d κ² ε⁻².

56
00:03:58,150 --> 00:04:02,315
To practice: prove that the minimum
of the potential is the solution.

57
00:04:02,695 --> 00:04:05,015
Explain why the transient is discarded.

58
00:04:05,395 --> 00:04:09,212
Compute how much the error falls if
the window is multiplied by four.

59
00:04:09,592 --> 00:04:11,511
And say what happens to the condition

60
00:04:11,511 --> 00:04:13,741
number when moving to
the normal equations.
